    "body": "Corvid Technologies plans to invest $95 million in a North Carolina expansion that will create 423 jobs and add a 400,000-square-foot manufacturing facility. The defense contractor will build the facility at its Mooresville headquarters to expand engineering and production of vehicle launch systems, Gov. Josh Stein announced earlier this week.\nWhat’s Related\n“In just two and a half years, we’ve grown from 8,000 square feet of manufacturing space to 800,000 square feet,” said Corvid President and CEO David Robinson. He said the company’s growth reflects demand from its customers.\nThe new jobs will include assemblers, electricians, engineers, production managers, technicians and testers. Annual salaries will average $81,550, compared with Iredell County’s average wage of $64,895, according to the state.\nRELATED STORIES: Caterpillar Plans $1 Billion Manufacturing Push in North Carolina | Amazon, Corning Bring Massive Fiber Expansion to North Carolina\nA new factory in Mooresville\nCorvid plans to complete the new facility by the end of 2030, according to Business North Carolina . The company already has 382 workers in Mooresville and plans to add the new positions over the next four years.\nFounded in 2004 as a small modeling and simulation firm, Corvid now works on projects ranging from missile launch systems to NASCAR. Its work includes design, prototyping, testing, and manufacturing.\nThe company considered Huntsville, Alabama, and Batavia, Ohio, before choosing Mooresville. Available workers, incentives, and infrastructure were among the factors in its decision, according to local reporting.\nState incentives tied to hiring and investment\nNorth Carolina’s Economic Investment Committee approved a grant that could reimburse Corvid up to $4,449,750 over 12 years. Payments will depend on state officials verifying that the company meets its annual hiring and investment targets.\nMooresville and Iredell County also committed a combined $6.4 million in property tax incentives, Business North Carolina reported. The company will receive $900,000 in workforce training.\nThe new agreement follows Corvid’s termination of a 2018 state incentive deal in September. The company repaid $126,745 in grants after telling the state it could not meet the earlier agreement’s hiring targets. Repaying that money was a condition of qualifying for the new grant, according to Business North Carolina.",
